Lab 16: Troubleshooting OpenStack Operations
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Inspect OpenStack service logs to identify error patterns.
Use OpenStack CLI tools to verify service health.
Analyze database connections and message queues for potential failures.
Prerequisites
A working OpenStack deployment (e.g., DevStack, Red Hat OpenStack Platform).
Administrative access (sudo/root privileges).
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and OpenStack components.
Installed OpenStack CLI tools (openstack, nova, neutron, etc.).
Access to log files (/var/log/<service>/).
Task 1: Inspect Service Logs for Error Patterns
Subtask 1.1: Locate OpenStack Service Logs
OpenStack services store logs in /var/log/<service>/. Common services include nova, neutron, keystone, and glance.

List available log directories:

ls /var/log | grep -E 'nova|neutron|keystone|glance'
View logs for a specific service (e.g., Nova):

sudo tail -f /var/log/nova/nova-api.log
Expected Outcome:
You should see real-time logs from the nova-api service. Look for ERROR or WARNING entries.

Troubleshooting Tip:
If logs are not updating, verify the service is running:

sudo systemctl status nova-api
Subtask 1.2: Filter Logs by Error Severity
Use grep to filter logs by error level.

Extract ERROR entries from Nova logs:

sudo grep -i "error" /var/log/nova/nova-api.log | less
Count occurrences of an error:

sudo grep -i "timeout" /var/log/nova/nova-compute.log | wc -l
Expected Outcome:
A filtered list of error messages. High counts may indicate persistent issues.

Task 2: Check Service Health Using CLI Tools
Subtask 2.1: Verify OpenStack Service Status
Use the openstack CLI to check service health.

List all OpenStack services:

openstack compute service list
Check Neutron networking agents:

openstack network agent list
Expected Outcome:
A table showing service status (up or down). Investigate any down services.

Troubleshooting Tip:
Restart a failed service:

sudo systemctl restart nova-compute
Subtask 2.2: Test API Endpoints
Ensure APIs are responsive.

Check Keystone (identity service) endpoint:

openstack token issue
Test Glance (image service) API:

openstack image list
Expected Outcome:
Successful API responses. Errors may indicate connectivity or authentication issues.

Task 3: Analyze Database and Message Queue Health
Subtask 3.1: Check Database Connectivity
OpenStack uses MariaDB/MySQL. Verify connections.

Log in to the database:

sudo mysql -u root -p
List OpenStack databases:

SHOW DATABASES LIKE '%nova%';
Expected Outcome:
A list of OpenStack databases (e.g., nova, keystone). Connection failures suggest database issues.

Subtask 3.2: Inspect RabbitMQ Message Queues
OpenStack uses RabbitMQ for messaging.

List RabbitMQ nodes:

sudo rabbitmqctl cluster_status
Check queue health:

sudo rabbitmqctl list_queues
Expected Outcome:
Active queues with pending messages. Stale queues may indicate service stalls.

Troubleshooting Tip:
Purge stuck queues:

sudo rabbitmqctl purge_queue <queue_name>
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Identified errors in OpenStack logs using grep and log inspection.
Verified service health via OpenStack CLI commands.
Diagnosed database and message queue issues affecting operations.
Next Steps:

Document recurring errors for root-cause analysis.
Refer to OpenStack upstream documentation for specific error resolutions.
